\par\par \uc2 Magazine Desk; SECTMM\par \b \uc2 The 24-Hour Workday\b0\par\par\uc2 By Malia Wollan \par \uc2 2,262 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 32\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Supercommuters, digital nomads, a robot-human nursing team. Here's what a day is like in the lives of nine 'flexible' workers.\par \par \uc2 The new era of flexible work has blurred the boundaries that define a workday. For information workers who spend most of their time on computers, the pandemic-era disruptions have created new geographic and temporal freedoms -- permanent three-day weekends, surfing while colleagues sleep, working from the couch, moving to the countryside, taking breaks to spend time with children. Some have done away with their offices and commutes completely. But the benefits of flexibility are not evenly distributed. For low-wage workers paid by the hour or the gig, this new era has tended to mean working increasingly unpredictable hours for more uncertain pay.\par \par \uc2 6 a.m. to 9 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Sophie Gil, 27,\par \par \uc2 is a digital nomad.\par \par \uc2 Gil is currently in Ahangama, Sri Lanka, some 9,000 miles away from and 12\u189\'00\'BD hours ahead of her co-workers in San Francisco. ''I didn't want to be bound to any time zone,'' she says.\par \par \uc2 Gil works for a social media company that operates asynchronously, meaning employees can do their jobs from anywhere at any time.\par \par \uc2 Irum Rashid-Jones, 42,\par \par \uc2 works FLEXIBLE HOURS.\par \par \uc2 Rashid-Jones founded and owns an electrical-services company in Dallas together with her husband. She is also a career coach for professional women and business owners in the construction industry.\par \par \uc2 Before the pandemic, Rashid-Jones did all her coaching meetings in person, but she now coaches people all over the country on her phone or laptop.\par \par \uc2 Meghan Folsom, 39,\par \par \uc2 is a supercommuter.\par \par \uc2 Folsom is a founder of a jewelry marketing company who moved to a small town in upstate New York early in the pandemic. Even when co-workers started returning regularly to the company's Manhattan office and showroom, Folsom decided not to move back.\par \par \uc2 Several times a month, Folsom wakes up before dawn to start her three-hour commute into the city. She drives 20 minutes to an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  station, takes a two-hour train to Penn Station, gets on the subway heading downtown and walks five blocks to her office.\par \par \uc2 9 a.m. to 12 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Cristina Lara-Agudelo, 26,\par \par \uc2 works a four-day week.\par \par \uc2 Lara-Agudelo works as a manager at a nonprofit in Denver that started piloting a four-day workweek in May 2020. This winter she decided to spend her work-free Fridays learning to snowboard.\par \par \uc2 The four-day-workweek model asks employees to do 40 hours of work more efficiently in 32 hours while keeping their full-time salaries. During her first summer of perpetual three-day weekends, Lara-Agudelo visited 10 national parks.\par \par \uc2 Richard Patrych, 27,\par \par \uc2 is a gig worker.\par \par \uc2 Patrych walks dogs for a living, sometimes as many as 15 a day. He finds work on Rover and Wag, two apps that specialize in pet services. If he has even an hour free, he'll go on an app and try to pick up an additional gig. He often walks 30,000 steps a day.\par \par \uc2 A 2021 \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  survey found that 30 percent of 18-to-29-year-olds have earned money through a gig platform. Among active gig workers of all ages, 31 percent reported that gigs were their main source of income over the past year.\par \par \uc2 Laroi Sutherland, 53,\par \par \uc2 is an independent contractor.\par \par \uc2 Sutherland is a long-haul truck driver who travels more than 3,000 miles per week -- equivalent to a coast-to-coast drive -- carrying frozen meat and vegetables in his bright red, refrigerated semi truck.\par \par \uc2 As an independent contractor, Sutherland is free to set his own schedule. But to make enough money, he needs to string together as many cargo pickups and drop-offs as he can, and he is often on the road for up to six weeks at a time before returning home to Conway, Ark.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Harris Whaley, 30,\par \par \uc2 works entirely from home.\par \par \uc2 Harris Whaley got a fully remote job as a project manager for a disability advocacy organization in 2021 and says she'll never go back to an office.\par \par \uc2 Some 11 percent of Americans work fully remotely today, compared with just 2 percent in 2019. At the height of remote work in May 2020, more than 60 percent of U.S. employees worked entirely from home.\par \par \uc2 Sophie Gil, 27,\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA MARKETER\par \par \uc2 Gil has lived in 12 countries since she started her fully remote job in April 2020. She often finds a rental near the beach and surfs in the morning, while it's still nighttime for her colleagues in California.\par \par \uc2 She works almost entirely through what researchers call stigmergic coordination, working across time and space through shared tools rather than through direct, real-time communication.\par \par \uc2 12 p.m. to 3 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Harris Whaley, 30,\par \par \uc2 DISABILITY RIGHTS ADVOCATE\par \par \uc2 Harris Whaley has cerebral palsy and an autoimmune condition. She says remote work creates new opportunities for the disabled community. According to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , the number of employed adults with disabilities hit a record high last year.\par \par \uc2 Working from home has given Harris Whaley more control over how she uses her time and her body. Some afternoons Harris Whaley naps, goes to the gym or -- more frequently these days -- visits the doctor, because she and her husband are expecting their first child in May.\par \par \uc2 Richard Patrych, 27,\par \par \uc2 DOG WALKER\par \par \uc2 Patrych started dog walking in 2020 after graduating from college during the pandemic. He was living with his elderly parents and needed money to get his own apartment, a move he hoped would help keep them safer.\par \par \uc2 At first, he worked as an \uc2 Instacart\uc2  shopper, but he found dog walking to be much more satisfying. He gets to spend his days outside. Plus, he points out, he likes dogs.\par \par \uc2 Sophie Gil, 27,\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA MARKETER\par \par \uc2 The one thing Gil needs is fast internet. Before moving to a new country, she asks for screenshots of internet speed tests at her rental and any nearby co-working spaces.\par \par \uc2 Because a majority of her team is asleep in other time zones while she works in a Sri Lankan beach town, Gil can focus without being interrupted by incoming emails, \uc2 Slack\uc2  messages and meetings.\par \par \uc2 Cristina Lara-Agudelo, 26,\par \par \uc2 NONPROFIT MANAGER\par \par \uc2 ''At first, cramming five days of work into four days was difficult,'' Lara-Agudelo says. Her entire organization received training on how to have fewer, more organized meetings.\par \par \uc2 With a three-day weekend, Lara-Agudelo finds she has time to plan adventures, see friends and also attend to chores like laundry and grocery shopping.\par \par \uc2 3 p.m. to 6 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Meghan Folsom, 39,\par \par \uc2 MARKETING EXECUTIVE\par \par \uc2 Folsom will often spend the night near her office in Manhattan in order to have two consecutive days with her team. But some days she'll do the full six-hour round trip so she can be home in time to put her 3-year-old son to sleep.\par \par \uc2 Even before the pandemic, the number of what researchers call ''supercommuters'' -- people who commute longer than 90 minutes one-way -- was rising, particularly around cities with expensive housing markets. In 2019, there were already more than 4.6 million nationwide.\par \par \uc2 There is no national data yet about supercommuters like Folsom: people who moved dozens, sometimes hundreds of miles from their offices during Covid and now make epic journeys back.\par \par \uc2 Laroi Sutherland, 53,\par \par \uc2 TRUCK DRIVER\par \par \uc2 After the pandemic hit, Sutherland started cooking in his cab to avoid restaurants. He bought a slow cooker and a microwave. He slow-cooks his dinner in a device called a lunchbox oven powered by the truck's 12-volt lighter plug.\par \par \uc2 Sutherland got wireless internet and a printer and generally fixed up his cab to feel more homelike, which made it easier to be on the road for even longer stretches. ''I'm a house and an office all on wheels now,'' he says.\par \par \uc2 Irum Rashid-Jones, 42,\par \par \uc2 CAREER COACH\par \par \uc2 Rashid-Jones's new flexibility means she often takes a break in the afternoon to spend time with her two children.\par \par \uc2 She might take her 14-year-old son to basketball practice or to the river near their house in northeast Texas to catch green sunfish.\par \par \uc2 6 p.m. to 9 p.m.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Harris Whaley, 30,\par \par \uc2 DISABILITY RIGHTS ADVOCATE\par \par \uc2 When Harris Whaley worked in person as a pediatric speech language pathologist, she would come home exhausted from hours on her feet. Not anymore.\par \par \uc2 She has enough energy left at the end of the day to go grocery shopping and cook dinner. Last year, in her free time, Harris Whaley wrote a children's book about disability and ableism that will be published in May.\par \par \uc2 Joanie Glasgow, 36,\par \par \uc2 works alongside a robot.\par \par \uc2 Last August, Glasgow was hired as a lead nurse on the night shift at a hospital near Detroit, often caring for patients with heart or lung disease. One of her new colleagues was a four-and-a-half-foot-tall robot.\par \par \uc2 The robot fetches equipment and supplies for the nurses and meals for patients, navigating crowded hallways and returning to its dock to charge when it's running out of power.\par \par \uc2 Trevor Crane, 43,\par \par \uc2 is an app-based shift worker.\par \par \uc2 Crane is an actor in an upcoming Off Off Broadway play. To help pay the bills, he picks up hourly work on an app called Hyr that promises employers it will help ''find extra staff in 15 minutes.''\par \par \uc2 Through the app, Crane has found shifts in bartending, catering and retail. He often does not know where he's working or for how long until the night before.\par \par \uc2 9 p.m. to 12 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Laroi Sutherland, 53,\par \par \uc2 TRUCK DRIVER\par \par \uc2 Delivering perishable food as part of a flexible supply chain means Sutherland's schedule is arrhythmic and his income unpredictable. Some weeks, he drives all night and sleeps all day; others he'll drive all day and sleep all night.\par \par \uc2 At the end of a shift, Sutherland draws the curtains in his cab and reads the Bible for 30 minutes before falling asleep. He is also making his way through ''Accounting for Non-accountants.''\par \par \uc2 Sophie Gil, 27,\par \par \uc2 SOCIAL MEDIA MARKETER\par \par \uc2 One day a week, Gil works late so she can meet with far-flung colleagues in real time.\par \par \uc2 She says being a digital nomad suits her because of her upbringing; her father is Mexican, her mother is Puerto Rican and she grew up in Italy and went to college in the United States.\par \par \uc2 In a few months, she'll move again. Next up: India. ''I don't necessarily think that where you're from is where you should be,'' Gil says.\par \par \uc2 Richard Patrych, 27,\par \par \uc2 DOG WALKER\par \par \uc2 Even though the apps take up to 40 percent of what he makes, Patrych says he has been able to pay his bills and not stress about money. But he married in December, which has him thinking about the future. Someday he wants to have children and be able to save money, goals that currently feel out of reach.\par \par \uc2 Because gig workers are considered independent contractors even when they work full time, Patrych receives no benefits; he accesses health care through Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Irum Rashid-Jones, 42,\par \par \uc2 Career coach\par \par \uc2 After dinner with her family, Rashid-Jones returns to work, either settling on her couch or walking the few blocks back to her office. She often works until midnight.\par \par \uc2 Workers used to be most productive before and after lunch, but doing a third sprint in the evening became more common during Covid, according to a 2022 study by \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  researchers, who called this pattern the ''triple peak'' day.\par \par \uc2 12 a.m. to 6 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Joanie Glasgow, 36, NURSE\par \par \uc2 By taking on some of their work, the robots give nurses like Glasgow more time to spend with patients. Over the past nine months, two robots at Glasgow's hospital have performed 13,276 tasks, according to the supplier Diligent Robotics.\par \par \uc2 According to a trade group, North American companies last year ordered a record-breaking 44,196 robots, many of which will work alongside humans as ''cobots.''\par \par \uc2 Trevor Crane, 43, ACTOR\par \par \uc2 Crane says he is accustomed to picking up odd, last-minute jobs; once he was paid to dress in a bigfoot costume to promote a television series. He likes that he can fit paid work around his acting.\par \par \uc2 The rise of apps for hourly work, sometimes described as the ''gig-ification'' of shift work, is the newest way that labor supply is being linked to fluctuations in consumer demand, leading to unpredictable schedules. One study of shift workers found that hours varied by an average of 32 percent from week to week.\par \par \uc2 Cristina Lara-Agudelo, 26,\par \par \uc2 NONPROFIT MANAGER\par \par \uc2 Lara-Agudelo used to work long hours at a financial-services company in New York City. She felt as if the only thing she did was work. On a four-day week, she travels more and goes to see concerts at night.\par \par \uc2 Employees who work four days a week report better mental health, more sleep and less stress, according to a 2022 study; 15 percent of the 2,900 employees surveyed said ''no amount of money'' would persuade them to go back to working a full week.\par \par \uc2 (MM32);  (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SAWYER ROQUE) (MM34);  (PHOTOGRAPHS BY PARTICIPANTS) (MM35; MM36; MM37; MM38; MM39; MM40; MM41);  (PHOTOGRAPHS BY SAWYER ROQUE; PARTICIPANTS) (MM42; MM43; MM44; MM45) This article appeared in print on page MM32, MM33, MM34, MM35, MM36, MM37, MM38, MM39, MM40, MM41, MM42, MM43, MM44, MM45. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230416ej4g0006d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
