\par\par \uc2 Magazine Desk; SECTMM\par \b \uc2 The Future of Work\b0\par\par\uc2 183 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Three years after the pandemic upended our working lives, ''flexibility'' is the buzzword. Nearly half of Americans now work from home at least part of the week, which means managing work time and personal time all the time. Even work that can't be done from home has become less rigid: ''Flextime'' allows some hourly workers to choose their shifts, and an explosion of gig platforms has made it possible for more people to control their time. There's no doubt that many Americans like this new flexibility: In a 2021 Harvard Business Review report, nearly 60 percent of ''knowledge workers'' said that it was more important than salary or other benefits. But this way of working can have its downsides. Some ''office workers'' feel that their 9-to-5 has turned into a 24/7 grind. And for low-income workers, variability in scheduling has often been accompanied by workplace monitoring, unpredictability and reduced benefits. This issue explores the new freedoms of flexible work and its costs.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page MM19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230416ej4g00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
