\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Labor Department Cites Dollar General as a 'Severe Violator' of Safety Standards\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Corkery \par \uc2 1,098 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Since January 2017, \uc2 OSHA\uc2  has cited the company for 111 instances of workplace safety violations and imposed more than $15.5 million in penalties.\par \par \uc2 For years, the Labor Department has operated a Severe Violator Enforcement Program, which targets companies with unsafe working conditions like manufacturers or construction firms with a high number of injuries or deaths.\par \par \uc2 In September, the department's \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  unit widened the scope of the program to include any type of company that willfully or repeatedly violated safety standards. The first to be added under the program's expanded scope: \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 , one of the nation's fastest-growing retailers.\par \par \uc2 Since January 2017, \uc2 OSHA\uc2  has inspected more than 270 \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  stores and found 111 instances of workplace safety violations. The agency has also imposed more than $15.5 million in penalties during that period, according to data provided by a White House official.\par \par \uc2 OSHA\uc2  inspectors have found issues such as obstructed fire exits and boxes of merchandise cluttering the aisles or stacked precariously high.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, federal inspectors have gone into a store to demand that a hazard be fixed, only to find in a follow-up visit that the problem was still there, according to two federal officials who spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss the violations.\par \par \uc2 The fines represent a tiny fraction of the billions of dollars in sales that \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  generates every year. And until recently, the officials said, the company was not willing to engage with \uc2 OSHA\uc2  about resolving these issues broadly, and it has contested many of the penalties.\par \par \uc2 ''What we have found time and time again at \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  stores is that there are obvious, preventable hazards that are putting workers at risk,'' Douglas L. Parker, assistant secretary of labor for occupational safety and health, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The company said that ''we regularly review and refine our safety programs, and reinforce them through training, ongoing communication, recognition and accountability.''\par \par \uc2 ''When we learn of situations where we have failed to live up to this commitment,'' it added, ''we work to timely address the issue and ensure that the company's expectations regarding safety are clearly communicated, understood and implemented.''\par \par \uc2 OSHA\uc2  and \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  are now in the very early stages of a settlement discussion to resolve the penalties and come up with a plan to improve certain conditions in the retailer's stores, the two federal officials said. \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  did not comment on the settlement talks.\par \par \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 , in many ways, is an obvious target for the Biden administration as it seeks to burnish its pro-worker image.\par \par \uc2 Based in Tennessee, \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  has been expanding its network of stores at a rapid clip, opening thousands of locations during the pandemic. With 19,000 stores in the United States, \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  operates twice as many locations as \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and Target combined.\par \par \uc2 Even as its sales and profits soar, \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  has faced mounting criticism from worker advocates and left-leaning think tanks over wages that are among the lowest in the retail and service industry and for failing to take basic precautions to prevent crime in its stores.\par \par \uc2 Despite the criticism, \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  remains a profitable juggernaut that has been filling a void in rural areas and impoverished urban neighborhoods, where often there aren't many other retailers selling inexpensive food and household staples.\par \par \uc2 During the five-year period that \uc2 OSHA\uc2  has cited the company for violations, \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 's share price has more than doubled.\par \par \uc2 ''I am glad \uc2 OSHA\uc2  is not waiting for a tragedy to occur to document these hazards,'' said Joseph McCartin, a labor historian at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 . ''But at the same time what is being revealed by the numerous \uc2 OSHA\uc2  inspections and mounting fees is that \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  doesn't care.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. McCartin said \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  was a case study in how \uc2 OSHA\uc2 's leverage was limited by the size of the fines that Congress had authorized it to impose. \uc2 OSHA\uc2  can charge a company up to $15,600 per day for up to 30 days for failure to address an unsafe condition.\par \par \uc2 ''When you have corporate scofflaws, which \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  has shown itself to be, the system needs to be strengthened,'' Mr. McCartin said.\par \par \uc2 The safety issues go deeper than a few rogue stores, federal officials said in interviews. \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 's business model relies on lightly staffed and relatively small box stores with high sales volume. Often there are not enough employees to unload a delivery truck and immediately stock the shelves, Mr. Parker said. That means inventory can linger in the aisles, creating hazards for employees and customers.\par \par \uc2 ''A business model should not be built on violating the law,'' Anita Dunn, a senior adviser to President Biden, said in an interview about \uc2 OSHA\uc2 's findings on \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 . ''The reality is that workers in this country are entitled to certain protections by law, and this administration will enforce that.''\par \par \uc2 Inclusion in the severe violators program means \uc2 OSHA\uc2  officials can inspect a store at random, even if the agency has not received a specific complaint about working conditions. Some of \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 's stores are among 462 workplaces in that program.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  penalties have come in the past two years under the Biden administration. More broadly, Mr. Biden has bolstered \uc2 OSHA\uc2 's enforcement by increasing its inspection staff by 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 The other large discount retailer, \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2 , which also operates the \uc2 Family Dollar\uc2  chain, has also been cited repeatedly for safety issues. Since 2017, according to data provided by a White House official, \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  has been cited for 90 violations and fined $14 million, while \uc2 Family Dollar\uc2  has been cited 54 times and fined $5 million.\par \par \uc2 ''A safe workplace for our associates is our highest priority,'' a \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  spokeswoman said. ''We are focused on maintaining a safe environment that complies with all health and safety regulations at our facilities. To that end, we continually enhance our safety programs and protocols.''\par \par \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2 , which is not in the severe violators program, has made some improvements to staffing and taken other measures, but it is still in discussions with \uc2 OSHA\uc2  about a more comprehensive safety plan, the two federal officials said.\par \par \uc2 Among the issues Dollar General has been cited for: obstructed fire exits, and aisles cluttered with boxes of goods. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SIMON SIMARD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230329ej3t0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
