\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 McConnell Is Back at Home After Release From Rehab\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephanie Lai \par \uc2 304 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Senate minority leader left the rehabilitation facility on Saturday and said he would be working from home for the next few days. He had been injured in a fall earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Kentucky Republican and minority leader who suffered a concussion in a serious fall earlier this month, was released from inpatient rehabilitation on Saturday, according to a statement he released Saturday evening.\par \par \uc2 ''I want to sincerely thank everyone for all the kind wishes,'' Mr. McConnell said in the statement. ''I'm happy to say I finished inpatient physical therapy earlier today, and I'm glad to be home.''\par \par \uc2 He added that he would be working from home for the next few days and would return in person soon, but did not say when he would be back at the Senate, which is in session next week.\par \par \uc2 According to a McConnell aide, the minority leader will consult with his physical therapists on a return date.\par \par \uc2 Mr. McConnell, 81, had a concussion and was hospitalized at George Washington University Hospital earlier this month, after tripping at a fund-raising dinner at the Waldorf Astoria hotel on Pennsylvania Avenue in Washington. The medical team discovered during Mr. McConnell's hospitalization that he had also suffered a ''minor rib fracture'' in the fall.\par \par \uc2 He was released from hospital observation less than a week later and began inpatient rehabilitation, which an aide said was a common practice for patients to regain strength after a hospital stay.\par \par \uc2 The former majority leader and the longest-serving Senate Republican leader in history had polio as a child, which left him with a limp. He has had other serious falls in the past.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230326ej3q0005f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
