\par\par \uc2 THE HUNT\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 A Pennsylvania Couple Decide to Make Their California Dreaming a Reality\b0\par\par\uc2 By Livia Albeck-Ripka \par \uc2 866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jennifer and Scott Adams fell in love with California in 2015, when their daughter began attending \uc2 Pepperdine University\uc2  in Malibu. The couple, who lived in West Chester, Pa., loved it so much that they floated the idea of following her there.\par \par \uc2 But their two younger children were still in school in Pennsylvania. And Mr. Adams, an executive director in cybersecurity for \uc2 Comcast\uc2 , was tied down by his job.\par \par \uc2 Then, in 2017, their middle son also left for Pepperdine. By 2021, their third child, a high school senior, was hoping to do the same.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic, too, had changed the landscape: Mr. Adams was largely working remotely and was free to move.\par \par \uc2 ''There was nothing really keeping us in West Chester,'' Ms. Adams said. ''I was superexcited to just do something new.''\par \par \uc2 They knew they wanted to live near Pepperdine, a college known for its picturesque spot on the Pacific Coast Highway. But they realized they would have to settle for a smaller space, at a higher price.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, the couple had a 2,400-square-foot, four-bedroom, two-and-a-half-bathroom home. Their mortgage payment was $2,600 a month. ''We thought, are we going to be able to swing it?'' Ms. Adams said. ''That's why we initially thought we would rent, and not buy.''\par \par \uc2 In April 2022, after more than two decades as homeowners, the couple put their house on the market. It sold for $742,500 -- more than $100,000 above asking price -- after drawing 15 offers. They began their search for a rental that same month, focusing on areas north of the university, including Calabasas, Agoura Hills, Oak Park and Thousand Oaks. Their son Matthew, 23, was at law school, and planned to move in with them. Henry, 19, would be starting at Pepperdine, living on campus, that fall.\par \par \uc2 ''We needed to be near Pepperdine,'' Ms. Adams said. They also wanted at least three bedrooms, two bathrooms, a yard, ample parking, storage space and enough room for Mr. Adams to work from home.\par \par \uc2 Among their options:\par \par \uc2 1. Modern Townhouse Condo\par \par \uc2 This 1,615-square-foot townhouse, with three bedrooms and two and a half bathrooms, was in Agoura Hills, just 11 miles north of the university. It was modern, with high ceilings, quartz kitchen counters, spacious living areas and a two-car garage. The complex had a communal area with barbecues and a spa, and was opposite a park. Storage, however, was limited, and there was no yard. The rent was $3,795 a month.\par \par \uc2 2. Spacious Two-Story with a Pool\par \par \uc2 This 2,318-square-foot house, with five bedrooms and two and a half bathrooms, was in Thousand Oaks, about 20 miles northwest of campus. It had a balcony and a courtyard. There was a heated swimming pool and spa, a three-car garage and plenty of street parking. The rent was $4,850 a month.\par \par \uc2 3. Cozy One-Story with a Yard\par \par \uc2 This 1,607-square-foot house, with four bedrooms and two bathrooms, was in Oak Park, about 14 miles northwest of the university. It had curb appeal, with a charming exterior and fruit trees in the yard. Inside, it had a fireplace, new bathrooms and a new kitchen, but there was no refrigerator and space was tight. Still, the house had a two-car garage, and there was plenty of street parking. Also, there were hiking trails nearby. The rent was $5,000 a month.\par \par \uc2 Their Choice\par \par \uc2 Ms. Adams had been sure her husband and sons would prefer the two-story house in Thousand Oaks, because of its pool and fifth bedroom, which could be used as an office. But when they saw it together, the family agreed that it lacked warmth and character.\par \par \uc2 They liked the condo, which felt clean, modern and surprisingly spacious. It was also closer to campus, but they really wanted a yard, and more storage space. They also felt that parking their cars in the neighborhood would be close to impossible.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Adams was disheartened after viewing several apartments, but as soon they pulled up to the Oak Park home, he breathed a sigh of relief. ''She really had a charm about her,'' he said. The neighborhood, surrounded by mountains and just a 25-minute drive from campus, felt instantly welcoming, he said. The family fell in love with the fireplace and the lemon, plum and pomegranate trees in the yard.\par \par \uc2 At first, the couple planned to stay in their rental for just a year, but the housing market in Southern California, as well as not knowing exactly where their children will end up, has made them reluctant to buy.\par \par \uc2 And while downsizing was painful at first, they have come to enjoy living in a smaller home. ''We really don't need as much space as we thought,'' Mr. Adams said.\par \par \uc2 Email thehunt@nytimes.com\par \par \uc2 The Renters Scott and Jennifer Adams and their sons, Matthew, left, and Henry, and their dog, Catcher. (PHOTOGRAPH BY BETH COLLER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page RE3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230326ej3q0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
