\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Bronx Man Convicted in Emergency Medical Worker's Death\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chelsia Rose Marcius and Liset Cruz \par \uc2 1,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jose Gonzalez was under the influence of drugs in 2017 when he commandeered the vehicle, running over Yadira Arroyo, a New York City emergency medical worker.\par \par \uc2 Six years after Yadira Arroyo, a New York City emergency medical worker, was crushed to death with her own ambulance, a Bronx jury convicted the man who commandeered the vehicle and ran her over.\par \par \uc2 The man, Jose Gonzalez, was found guilty of first degree murder on Wednesday. The jury returned its verdict after less than two days of deliberations. He is expected to be sentenced on April 5.\par \par \uc2 Members of Ms. Arroyo's family cried and huddled as the verdict was read. Some of the roughly 15 members of the city's Fire Department who were inside the courtroom embraced. Mr. Gonzalez, who was surrounded by about a dozen police officers, was led out in handcuffs.\par \par \uc2 ''We finally, finally get justice for her,'' Darcel Clark, the Bronx district attorney, said at a brief news conference outside the Bronx Hall of Justice.\par \par \uc2 The verdict ''means everything to me and to this family,'' Ms. Clark said.\par \par \uc2 The trial had been repeatedly delayed after Mr. Gonzalez was considered mentally unfit to stand trial. At the time of Ms. Arroyo's death on March 16, 2017, Mr. Gonzalez had tested positive for marijuana and a hallucinogen.\par \par \uc2 George Suminski, a prosecutor for the Bronx district attorney, said during closing arguments on Tuesday that Mr. Gonzalez was aware of his own movements and surroundings. He said Mr. Gonzalez also saw Ms. Arroyo, proving his actions were intentional.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gonzalez's lawyer, Richard Barton, resisted that idea, arguing his client could not distinguish fantasy from reality.\par \par \uc2 ''He was delusional at the time he got into the ambulance,'' Mr. Barton said.\par \par \uc2 The verdict ended a case that once roiled the city. The death of Ms. Arroyo, a mother of five sons, put a spotlight on the risks that emergency medical workers face when they encounter people in the throes of crisis. Last year, Lieutenant Alison Russo-Elling, a 24-year veteran just shy of retirement, was stabbed to death by a man outside her station in Queens.\par \par \uc2 The murder also ignited renewed debate over the so-called dangerousness standard in New York's bail law, which prevents judges from considering criminal history when they set the terms of pretrial release.\par \par \uc2 Three weeks before Ms. Arroyo's murder, Mr. Gonzalez had been accused of kicking out the back window of a police van and trying to punch an officer. The judge in that case had released Mr. Gonzalez, who was already awaiting trial on separate assault charges -- a decision that was put under the microscope following Ms. Arroyo's death.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gonzalez encountered Ms. Arroyo, 44, a 14-year Fire Department veteran known as Yari, while she was driving an ambulance along White Plains Road in the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 Her partner, Monique Williams, was in the passenger seat as they rolled up to a red light near Watson Avenue, according to surveillance video played in court on Monday. They were working overtime to answer a call from a pregnant woman who needed medical aid, Ms. Williams testified during the trial.\par \par \uc2 About that time, Mr. Gonzalez darted out of an apartment building, ran up to the ambulance and hopped onto the back bumper, according to another surveillance video. He peered in to the back window before jumping down and making a beeline for the sidewalk, the video shows.\par \par \uc2 He then ran up to a teenage boy and demanded his backpack, according to prosecutors and dashcam video. The boy handed over the bag, then promptly flagged down the ambulance for help.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Arroyo stepped down from the driver's seat and told Mr. Gonzalez to return the backpack, Ms. Williams testified on Feb. 14. Mr. Gonzalez instead went up to Ms. Williams and complained that his hand hurt and said he needed help.\par \par \uc2 Then Mr. Gonzalez spun around, raced toward the ambulance and climbed into the open driver's side door. Ms. Arroyo attempted to pull him out as Ms. Williams fought for the steering wheel.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gonzalez abruptly threw the vehicle in reverse, trapping Ms. Arroyo underneath, prosecutors said. He then drove forward, dragging her into the intersection as he rounded the corner before crashing into a car.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Williams said she found her partner lying in the middle of the street, lifeless.\par \par \uc2 ''I ran over to her to try and get her up, and she didn't get up,'' she said, crying. ''She didn't move no more.''\par \par \uc2 During the trial, jurors weighed testimony regarding Mr. Gonzalez's mental state at the time of the killing. He had tested positive for phencyclidine, a hallucinogen that can induce a type of psychosis, according to Dr. Eric Goldsmith, a psychiatrist who interviewed Mr. Gonzalez hours after the incident. During that interview, Mr. Gonzalez also said he was being targeted by the Illuminati, an 18th-century secret society that figures in many conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 Even so, Dr. Goldsmith, who served as an expert witness for the Bronx district attorney's office, testified on Monday that Mr. Gonzalez still had awareness of his surroundings at the time of Ms. Arroyo's death.\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Gonzalez appeared anxious on the surveillance video, he was steady on his feet. He had also made consistent statements that night to Dr. Goldsmith, detectives and an assistant district attorney about why he needed an ambulance: Mr. Gonzalez told them he needed to get to a hospital so that he could receive medical treatment for his hand, Dr. Goldsmith said.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Barton, who played a portion of Mr. Gonzalez's interview with the police during closing arguments, told the jury his client was convinced of his version of reality: that there was no driver in the ambulance, that he needed to get into the vehicle to receive medical attention for his hand, and that he only exited the ambulance because he thought there was a bomb inside.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not going to jail. I didn't kill nobody,'' Mr. Gonzalez told detectives after Ms. Arroyo's killing.\par \par \uc2 Members of the New York City Fire Department outside court in support of their fallen colleague.; Jose Gonzalez was found guilty of first-degree murder in Ms. Arroyo's 2017 death. He is expected to be sentenced on April 5. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY GREGG VIGLIOTTI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Yadira Arroyo was fatally crushed with her ambulance. (PHOTOGRAPH BY FIRE DEPARTMENT OF NEW YORK) This article appeared in print on page A19. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230309ej3900045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
