\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A 120-Year-Old Company Is Leaving \uc2 Tesla\uc2  in the Dust\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ezra Dyer \par \uc2 1,306 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  had me convinced, for a while, that it was a cool company.\par \par \uc2 It made cars that performed animatronic holiday shows using their lights and power-operated doors. It came up with dog mode (a climate control system that stays running for dogs in a parked car), a GPS-linked air suspension that remembers where the speed bumps are and raises the car automatically and ''fart mode'' (in which the car makes fart sounds).\par \par \uc2 And, fundamentally, its cars had no competition. If you wanted an electric car that could go more than 250 miles between charges, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  was your only choice for the better part of a decade. The company's C.E.O., \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , came across as goofy and eccentric: You could build great cars and name each model such that the lineup spells ''SEXY.''\par \par \uc2 Or you would, if not for the party killers over at boring old \uc2 Ford\uc2 . \uc2 Ford\uc2  thwarted Mr. Musk's ''SEXY'' gambit by preventing \uc2 Tesla\uc2  from naming its small sedan the Model E, since that sounds a bit too much like a certain famous \uc2 Ford\uc2 , the Model T. So Mr. Musk went with Model 3, which either ruins the joke or elevates it, depending on how much you venerate \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . I count myself as a former admirer of Mr. Musk and \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , and in fact put a deposit on a Model 3 after my first drive of one.\par \par \uc2 But the more I dealt with \uc2 Tesla\uc2  as a reporter -- this was before Mr. Musk fired all the P.R. people who worked there -- the more skeptical I became. Any time I spoke to any people at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , there was a sense that they were terrified to say the wrong thing or anything at all. I wanted to know the horsepower of the Model 3 I was driving, and the result was like one of those oblique Mafia conversations in which nothing's stated explicitly, in case the feds are listening. I ended up saying, ''Well, I read that this car has 271 horsepower,'' and one \uc2 Tesla\uc2  person replied, ''I wouldn't disagree with that.'' This is not how healthy, functional companies answer simple factual questions.\par \par \uc2 That was back in 2017. In the years since, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's become even crankier, while its competition has loosened up. Public perception hasn't yet caught up with the reality of the situation. If you want to work for a flexible, modern company, you don't apply to \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . You apply to 120-year-old \uc2 Ford\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's veneer of irreverence conceals an inflexible core, an old-fashioned corporate autocracy. Consider \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's remote work policy, or lack thereof. Last year, Mr. Musk issued a decree that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  employees log 40 hours per week in an office -- and not a home office -- if they expected to keep their jobs. On \uc2 Indeed.com\uc2 , the question, ''Can you work remotely at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 ?'' includes answers like, ''No,'' and ''Absolutely not, they won't let it happen under any circumstances,'' and ''No, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  will work you until you lose everything.''\par \par \uc2 But on the other hand, the cars make fart noises. What a zany and carefree company!\par \par \uc2 Ford\uc2 's work-from-home rules for white-collar employees, meanwhile, sound straight out of Silicon Valley, in that the official corporate policy is that there is no official corporate policy -- it's up to the leaders of individual units to require in-person collaboration, or not, as situations dictate. There are new ''collaboration centers'' in lieu of cubicle farms, complete with food service and concierges. That's not the reality of daily work life for every person at \uc2 Ford\uc2  -- you can't exactly bolt together an F-150 from home -- but it's an attempt to provide some flexibility for as many people as possible.\par \par \uc2 Ford\uc2  also tends to make good on its promises, an area that's become increasingly fraught for \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . \uc2 Ford\uc2  said it would offer a hands-free driver assist system, and now it does, with BlueCruise; you can take your hands off the steering wheel when it is engaged on mapped sections of highway. \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's Full Self-Driving system is not hands-free in any situation, despite its name, and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  charges customers $15,000 for the feature on the promise that someday it will make the huge leap to fully autonomous driving.\par \par \uc2 If you want to pay $15,000 for a feature that's currently subject to a \uc2 National Highway Traffic Safety Administration\uc2  recall whose filing is titled ''Full Self-Driving Software May Cause Crash,'' don't let me stop you, but a \uc2 Tesla\uc2  engineer also recently testified that a company video purporting to show the system in flawless action was faked. This makes sense, given all the other very real videos of Full Self-Driving doing things like steering into oncoming traffic or braking to a complete stop on a busy street for no reason. \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's own website warns, ''The currently enabled features require a fully attentive driver, who has their hands on the wheel and is prepared to take over at any moment.'' So, full self-driving, except for that.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's long-promised new vehicles, like the Cybertruck and a new version of its Roadster, also keep getting delayed. The Cybertruck was unveiled in 2019, and on \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's most recent earnings call Mr. Musk admitted that it won't be in production this year, which is becoming an annual refrain. Sure, \uc2 Ford\uc2  sold only 15,617 electric F-150 Lightning pickups in 2022, but that beats the Cybertruck's sales by, let's see, 15,617. Besides stealing \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's market share on trucks, \uc2 Ford\uc2 's stealing its corporate impishness, too -- when the electric Mustang Mach-E was unveiled, \uc2 Ford\uc2  demonstrated its tailgating possibilities by filling its drainable front trunk (or frunk) with shrimp. ''Frunk shrimp'' became a meme, which surely tormented the emperor of try-hard social media posting, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Speaking of which: \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I will hazard the opinion that Mr. Musk's $44 billion purchase of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has not exactly burnished \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's reputation. Besides showcasing the questionable decision making inherent in paying that much for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Mr. Musk's heightened profile on the platform hasn't really done him any favors. For instance, when the bulk of your car company's sales are in blue states, is it helpful to tweet, ''My pronouns are Prosecute/Fauci''? Moreover, you'd think that the self-appointed class clown of corporate America would at least strive for a joke that eschews the hacky ''my pronouns are/I identify as'' construction. Maybe just go with ''Fauci makes me grouchy''? Elon, let's workshop this next time.\par \par \uc2 Maybe predictability isn't trendy, but if you buy a new car you'd probably like to think that its manufacturer won't cut the price by $13,000 the next week, thus destroying your car's resale value. And you might hope that features you pay for work on the day you pay for them, and not at some unspecified future date. Maybe you want a car from a company whose C.E.O. isn't indelibly associated with the product.\par \par \uc2 I just bought a Jeep, and I have no idea who the C.E.O. is there. That's cool with me.\par \par \uc2 Ezra Dyer is a columnist for Car and Driver magazine.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230307ej3700030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
