\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Judge Rebukes \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  For Labor Law Violations\b0\par\par\uc2 By Noam Scheiber \par \uc2 691 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The decision cited ''egregious and widespread misconduct,'' including illegal monitoring and firing of workers. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  signaled that it would appeal.\par \par \uc2 In a sweeping decision, an administrative judge in New York ruled on Wednesday that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had violated federal labor law dozens of times in responding to a union campaign in the Buffalo area shortly after the campaign began roughly 18 months ago.\par \par \uc2 Michael A. Rosas, a judge for the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , concluded that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had illegally monitored, disciplined and fired employees engaged in union organizing; added workers to stores to dilute support for the union; and promised new benefits to workers in an attempt to defuse support for the union.\par \par \uc2 The ruling mandates the reinstatement of seven Buffalo-area workers who the judge concluded were unlawfully discharged from the company, and back pay and damages to more than two dozen workers who the judge concluded had suffered retaliation that affected their compensation, such as a reduction of hours.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the judge ordered the chief executive of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , to read or be present for the reading of a notice, more than 10 pages long, promising to refrain from committing a series of labor law violations in the future, and to make and distribute a video of the reading.\par \par \uc2 Because of the company's ''egregious and widespread misconduct demonstrating a general disregard for the employees' fundamental rights,'' Judge Rosas wrote, it was necessary to issue a broad order requiring \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  ''to cease and desist from infringing in any other manner on rights guaranteed employees.''\par \par \uc2 ''This is truly a historic ruling,'' Gary Bonadonna Jr., the regional head of Workers United, the union organizing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , said in a statement. ''We will continue to fight and hold billionaires like \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  accountable for their actions. We will not rest until every \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  worker wins the right to organize.''\par \par \uc2 The ruling can be appealed to the labor board in Washington, and to federal court after that, and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  indicated that it might do so. ''We believe the decision and the remedies ordered are inappropriate given the record in this matter and are considering all options to obtain further legal review,'' the company said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ' response to the union campaign has gotten heightened scrutiny recently. Earlier on Wednesday, Senator Bernie Sanders of Vermont announced that he planned to hold a vote next week on whether to subpoena Mr. Schultz before the Senate labor committee. Mr. Schultz had declined an earlier invitation from Mr. Sanders to testify about the company's actions during the union campaign.\par \par \uc2 Last month, a federal judge ordered the reinstatement of a Michigan worker who was involved in union organizing when the company fired her in April.\par \par \uc2 The organizing campaign notched its first victory in Buffalo in 2021. Since then, more than 280 of the roughly 9,300 corporate-owned \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  locations in the United States have unionized. The ruling covers the period from August 2021 to July 2022, by which point the campaign had spread from the Buffalo area to dozens of stores nationwide.\par \par \uc2 In the early months of the campaign, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers complained that executives and other company officials were converging on Buffalo in an attempt to undermine their unionization effort.\par \par \uc2 Judge Rosas found that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had violated labor law by ''having high-ranking company officials make repeated and unprecedented visits to stores in order to more closely supervise, monitor or create the impression that employees' union activities are under surveillance.''\par \par \uc2 He also ordered the company to bargain with the union at a Buffalo-area location where the union lost an election in December 2021, concluding that the scope of the violations at the store tainted the vote and made a rerun of the election an ''insufficient'' remedy.\par \par \uc2 It is rare but not unprecedented for a judge to effectively order in a union upon concluding that it had support among workers but that a fair vote is nearly impossible.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks workers rallying outside a store in Buffalo in November. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LINDSAY DEDARIO/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230302ej320004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
