\par\par \uc2 VISUAL INVESTIGATIONS\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 U.S. Aid Worker Was Killed by Guided Missile, Video Shows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Thomas Gibbons-Neff and Malachy Browne \par \uc2 875 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 February 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A Times analysis suggests that an intentional strike, not an indiscriminate attack, most likely killed Pete Reed. It is unclear whether the attackers knew he was with a group of aid workers.\par \par \uc2 Roughly a minute after an American paramedic, Pete Reed, and a team of aid workers began tending to a wounded civilian in the Ukrainian city of Bakhmut on Feb. 2, they were attacked. Mr. Reed, a former U.S. Marine volunteering on the war's front lines, was killed, and several of his colleagues were wounded.\par \par \uc2 Volunteers at the scene initially attributed the strike to indiscriminate Russian shelling. But a frame-by-frame analysis of a video taken at the location -- and shared with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  -- shows that Mr. Reed, who was unarmed, died in a targeted strike by a guided missile almost certainly fired by Russian troops.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear if the Russians knew the group was made up of aid workers. But its convoy had markings that should have signaled to the Russians the type of vehicles they were hitting. One of the vehicles was clearly marked with a red cross, and the type of weapon used in the attack -- a laser-guided antitank missile -- is usually fired when a gunman sees and selects a target.\par \par \uc2 Still, the target in this case, a white \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2  van, did not have any clearly visible medical markings, and while the aid workers were unarmed at least one medic was wearing military-style camouflage.\par \par \uc2 The video shows Mr. Reed and the group of aid workers standing beside the white van, which they were using to transport humanitarian supplies. A missile flying parallel to the ground directly hits the van, destroying it and killing Mr. Reed.\par \par \uc2 The video was filmed on a smartphone by Erko Laidinen, an Estonian volunteer with an organization called Frontline Medics who was trailing just behind Mr. Reed.\par \par \uc2 The footage appears to show that the strike involved a Kornet antitank guided missile, which has a range of around three miles. Mr. Reed and the aid workers were at a slightly elevated position along a street that led toward the Russian front line, around two miles away.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Laidinen said that his vehicle's dash camera had also recorded the episode, and that the footage showed a second missile strike, which was aimed at another vehicle but missed its target. The footage has yet to be made public.\par \par \uc2 A volunteer named Roma, who was standing near Mr. Reed when the missile struck and who was wounded in the blast, told The Times in an interview that there had been no military units nearby. One of the vehicles at the scene was clearly marked as an ambulance, he said.\par \par \uc2 He provided only his given name because of safety concerns.\par \par \uc2 A photograph published by The Wall Street Journal shows an injured Norwegian medic running from the scene of the attack. It also shows the ambulance marked with a red cross on a white background across the street from where Mr. Reed and other volunteers were attacked.\par \par \uc2 Experts said the type of weapon used should have enabled the attacker to identify the nature of the target. With weapons such as these, ''you have an expectation that the firer is going to have the ability to differentiate between a medical worker and a combatant,'' said Marc Garlasco, a war crimes investigator who is in Poland training Ukrainian teams investigating war crimes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garlasco added that the episode required further investigation, but that on its face it was a ''potential war crime.''\par \par \uc2 A video of the aftermath shows the aid workers' white van destroyed by the attack. Debris is strewn around the area, and a body is lying lifeless on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Bakhmut, an industrial city surrounded by salt mines with a prewar population of around 70,000 people, has been under intense bombardment since the summer. In recent weeks, Russian troops have come increasingly close to encircling the city.\par \par \uc2 With a small population of civilians still present in the city, aid workers like Mr. Reed and his teammates have served as lifelines for people sheltering in basements without heat and with dwindling rations. On Monday, the Ukrainian military said it would no longer allow aid groups into the city.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Reed and his team were alerted to the wounded civilian by Ukrainian troops that had just returned from the area. The street had been under shelling or missile attack at some point: At least one other vehicle had been destroyed in the same area, though it was unclear when, Roma said.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian forces traverse the battlefield in all types of civilian vehicles, including privately owned sedans and school buses. It is therefore possible that Mr. Reed was targeted because his team had simply driven into a kill zone frequently targeted by Russian troops.\par \par \uc2 Natalia Yermak, Christiaan Triebert and John Ismay contributed reporting. Videos and images produced by Axel Boada.\par \par \uc2 Natalia Yermak, Christiaan Triebert and John Ismay contributed reporting. Videos and images produced by Axel Boada.\par \par \uc2 A Likely Intentional Strike (PHOTOGRAPH BY ERKO LAIDINEN, FRONTLINE MEDICS) This article appeared in print on page A8. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230215ej2f00051\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
