\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Disney\uc2  World Employees Turn Down Deal Offer\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brooks Barnes \par \uc2 771 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unions that represent about 32,000 full-time workers said the vote was overwhelmingly against the proposal, which would have raised pay by at least $1 an hour per year.\par \par \uc2 Add escalating labor tensions at Walt Disney World to the list of problems at the world's largest entertainment company.\par \par \uc2 Unions that represent about 32,000 full-time workers at Disney World -- ride operators, costumed performers, housekeepers, restaurant and shop employees, bus drivers, custodians -- said on Friday night that members had voted to reject \uc2 Disney\uc2 's offer for a new five-year contract. Matt Hollis, president of the Service Trades Council Union, a consortium of six unions, said that 96 percent of the votes cast went against \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Disney\uc2  can do better and must do better,'' Mr. Hollis said at a union event in Orlando, Fla.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Disney\uc2  said that its ''strong offer'' would provide more than 30,000 employees ''a nearly 10 percent on average raise immediately, as well as retroactive increased pay in their paychecks, and we are disappointed that those increases will now be delayed.''\par \par \uc2 The minimum starting wage for the \uc2 Disney\uc2  workers is currently $15. Florida's state minimum wage is $11, rising to $12 in the fall.\par \par \uc2 The company's offer would raise pay for the covered employees by at least $1 an hour per year, taking most workers to at least $20 an hour by 2026. About 13,800 of the workers would get a raise of more than $1 an hour in the first year. Under the proposal, certain workers (housekeepers, bus drivers, certain culinary employees) would reach $20 an hour in the first year. \uc2 Disney\uc2 's offer includes various perks, including an additional 401(k) plan and eight weeks of paid family leave.\par \par \uc2 The six unions want an immediate $3 an hour raise -- lifting the starting minimum to $18 an hour, a level that they say is necessary to address runaway housing costs and other rising living expenses in the Orlando area. The unions want $1 an hour more in every subsequent year. (Last month, some of the same unions won an $18 an hour starting wage for workers at the nearby Orange County Convention Center.)\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  will now need to return to the bargaining table.\par \par \uc2 The previous three-year contract expired in October. (\uc2 Disney\uc2  requested a five-year one as a replacement.) Since then, Disney World has been operating under an extension that prohibits the unionized workers from striking. It is unknown when the extension expires. Even when it does, members of the unions would still need to vote to approve a strike.\par \par \uc2 Contract talks started in August. Last month, \uc2 Disney\uc2  disclosed in a securities filing that its former chief executive, \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2 , who was fired in the fall, received a $20 million severance package, outraging some union members. \uc2 Disney\uc2  also revealed that another ousted executive received $10 million for roughly three months of work, or more than $176,000 a day.\par \par \uc2 The Walt Disney Company\uc2  needs to keep Disney World operating at full tilt to make up for losses in its nascent \uc2 Disney\uc2 + streaming division. Last year, Disney Parks, Experiences and Products generated $7.9 billion in operating profit. \uc2 Disney\uc2 's streaming unit lost about $4 billion.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Disney\uc2  is also contending with an activist investor, \uc2 Nelson Peltz\uc2 , who wants the company to put a magnifying glass on costs, refocus on profit growth and reinstate its dividend, all of which increase pressure on \uc2 Disney\uc2  to keep a lid on labor costs. \uc2 Disney\uc2  is loaded with debt -- more than $45 billion -- because of the pandemic and because of its $71.3 billion acquisition of \uc2 21st Century Fox\uc2  assets in 2019.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, \uc2 Disney\uc2  is still dealing with fallout from its condemnation of a new education law that opponents labeled ''Don't Say Gay.'' Among many things, the law prohibits discussion of sexual orientation and gender identity through the third grade in Florida classrooms and limits it for older students. After \uc2 Disney\uc2  criticized the law and paused political donations in the state, Florida lawmakers retaliated by ending Disney World's designation as a special tax district, a privilege that effectively allowed \uc2 Disney\uc2  to self-govern the 25,000-acre complex.\par \par \uc2 The Florida Legislature will convene a special session on Monday, part of which will be dedicated to establishing a new governing structure for Disney World.\par \par \uc2 The Magic Kingdom park at Disney World in Florida. Disney defended its contract offer to union members as ''strong.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY TED SHAFFREY/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230205ej250004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
