\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Nurses Extend Their Strike At 2 Hospitals in New York\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Otterman \par \uc2 823 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nurses returned to the picket lines on Tuesday at \uc2 Mount Sinai Hospital\uc2  and \uc2 Montefiore Medical Center\uc2 , which were functioning with a skeletal staff.\par \par \uc2 More than 7,000 nurses at \uc2 Mount Sinai Hospital\uc2  and \uc2 Montefiore Medical Center\uc2  returned to the picket lines on Tuesday as New York City's largest nurse strike in decades continued into a second day.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of nurses, wearing their bright red union hats and scarves, marched outside both hospitals Tuesday morning to call for improved wages and more nurses to care for patients, chanting, holding signs and blowing air horns. Inside the hospitals, a skeleton staff cared for reduced patient loads. The nurses said they worried that patient care was suffering inside, but that improving patient safety in the long term was one of the main reasons they went on strike.\par \par \uc2 ''We are very hopeful that we will get something settled soon,'' said \uc2 Judy Sheridan-Gonzalez\uc2 , a nurse on the negotiating committee at Montefiore. ''I really want us to go back.''\par \par \uc2 At Montefiore in the Bronx, nurses and management met at the bargaining table for continued talks on Tuesday morning. At \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 's main campus on Fifth Avenue in Manhattan, talks were stalled as of Tuesday evening, frustrating the nursing union negotiators, who said they wanted to return to the table as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 Nursing contracts between the \uc2 New York State Nursing Association\uc2  and about a dozen private city hospitals expired on Dec. 31. Since then, eight hospitals have come to tentative agreements or ratified them. Talks continue at three hospitals, and two are striking.\par \par \uc2 Nurses have been seeking higher wages, as well as assurances that management will regularly assign more nurses to care for patients. While wage demands have largely been met, the striking nurses are seeking more guarantees that they will not regularly be asked to care for more patients than they feel they can safely handle.\par \par \uc2 Nurses on the picket line at Montefiore described overcrowded, understaffed conditions that they hoped a new contract would address.\par \par \uc2 In the medical intensive care unit, the nurse Mary Powell said some of her patients should have one-on-one care, but often she has to attend to two or three patients instead. ''And you're just forced to make it work in order to make sure the patient stays alive. It's a lot of stress.''\par \par \uc2 Negotiations have been going on for four months, Ms. Sheridan-Gonzalez said, but movement toward union demands of enforceable staffing ratios and recruitment bonuses to compete with higher pay at nearby \uc2 NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital\uc2  only happened in the last few days.\par \par \uc2 To deal with the strike, the hospitals have called in traveling nurses and relied on medical residents and other hospital staff who remained at work. Montefiore has canceled all elective surgeries and procedures, and Mount Sinai has transferred patients to other facilities, including Mount Sinai West and Mount Sinai Morningside, where contract agreements were already reached.\par \par \uc2 Still, some patients and staff inside the hospitals described chaotic conditions, while others said the remaining staff were managing to handle the reduced patient load, at least for the moment.\par \par \uc2 Philip Ozuah, the president and chief executive at Montefiore, said in a letter to staff on Monday that he was deeply disappointed by the strike, and blamed the union for walking away from patients. ''In my opinion, this action was totally unnecessary, especially given how close we already were to a final agreement,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 That sentiment was met with fury from the striking nurses outside of Montefiore on Tuesday. ''We are disappointed in you,'' they shouted, adding that they just wanted the staff they needed to care for patients effectively.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 , management was also highly critical of the nurses' decision to strike, calling it reckless. Both hospitals are offering the same 19.1 percent increase in wages over three years settled on by all the hospitals that have so far reached agreements, including two other Mount Sinai Health System campuses.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2 , the two sides still have not agreed on enforcement language for patient-nurse ratios or increased seniority differentials to match pay at Mount Sinai Morningside and Mount Sinai West, union representatives said.\par \par \uc2 Talks have gone better elsewhere. The union has reached a tentative agreement with the \uc2 Brooklyn Hospital Center\uc2 , and ratified agreements at Mount Sinai Morningside, Mount Sinai West, NewYork-Presbyterian, \uc2 Maimonides Medical Center\uc2 , \uc2 Richmond University Medical Center\uc2 , \uc2 Flushing Hospital Medical Center\uc2  and BronxCare.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations continue with \uc2 Interfaith Medical Center\uc2 , Kingsbrook Jewish Medical Center and Wyckoff Heights Medical Center, all three of which are in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 Sean Piccoli and Christopher Alvarez contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Sean Piccoli and Christopher Alvarez contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 ''I really want us to go back,'' said Judy Sheridan-Gonzalez, a nurse picketing outside Montefiore. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEENAH MOON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230111ej1b0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\pi